   "date": "2018-07-24T10:57:27Z",
   "subject": "Re: Extract A- and B-allele Copy Number from defined genomic windows.",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\">Hi Joao,<div><br></div><div>yes, you can define breakpoints in the extract step (bypassing the built-in segmentation).</div><div>You need a data.frame file with 3 columns: chrom, start.pos end.pos.</div><div>Then, when you run sequenza.extract you use the object with the breaks option:</div><div><br></div><div>    my_breaks &lt;- read.table(“my_curated_bed_file.txt”, header=F, sep=“\\t”, stringsAsFactor=FALSE)</div><div>    colnames(my_breaks) &lt;- c(“chrom”, “start.pos”, “end.pos&quot;)</div><div>    test &lt;- sequenza.extract(my_seqz_file, breaks=my_breaks)<br><div><br></div><div>Unfortunately there isn’t a easy way to automatically provide the “right” segmentation for the 2 samples, I usually end up doing a visual inspection with chromosome.view and manually curate the breaks in the segments file</div><div><br></div><div>There are multi-sample segmentation options, but not with the allele frequency taken into account.</div><div><br></div><div>Francesco</div><div> </div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div>On 24 Jul 2018, at 11.37, Joao Miguel Alves &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">jmfernan...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:</div><br></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\" style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none\">Dear all,<div><br></div><div>I&#39;ve ran<span> </span><i><b>sequenza</b></i><span> </span>on multiple samples from the same tumor and realised that the &quot;segments&quot; printed in *segments.txt are not exactly the same between samples.</div><div>For instance:</div><div><br></div><div>SampleA:</div><div><span style=\"font-variant-ligatures:no-common-ligatures;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.721569);color:rgb(244,244,244);font-family:Monaco;font-size:10px\">chr1</span><span style=\"white-space:pre;font-variant-ligatures:no-common-ligatures;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.721569);color:rgb(244,244,244);font-family:Monaco;font-size:10px\">\t</span><span style=\"font-variant-ligatures:no-common-ligatures;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.721569);color:rgb(244,244,244);font-family:Monaco;font-size:10px\">861360</span><span style=\"white-space:pre;font-variant-ligatures:no-common-ligatures;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.721569);color:rgb(244,244,244);font-family:Monaco;font-size:10px\">\t</span><span style=\"font-variant-ligatures:no-common-ligatures;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.721569);color:rgb(244,244,244);font-family:Monaco;font-size:10px\">120529750</span><span style=\"white-space:pre;font-variant-ligatures:no-common-ligatures;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.721569);color:rgb(244,244,244);font-family:Monaco;font-size:10px\">\t</span><span style=\"font-variant-ligatures:no-common-ligatures;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.721569);color:rgb(244,244,244);font-family:Monaco;font-size:10px\">0.498945460101109</span><span style=\"white-space:pre;font-variant-ligatures:no-common-ligatures;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.7